    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Consistent splintering at the tips of the pins can be frustrating. To manage the grain, ensure you're cutting with the direction of the grain when driving the pins, and use a slightly darker cutting direction to help control the wood fibers. Check your chisel edge for excessive heat damage or binding, and consider honing or stoning your chisels to maintain a sharp edge. Additionally, try using a more gentle, sweeping motion to drive the chiseled pins, rather than applying too much pressure.",
      "source": "target",
