    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I've been honing the chisels on a fine diamond stone and they feel incredibly sharp, so I don't think it's the edge itself. It almost feels like the wood is resisting or just giving way unexpectedly right at the end of the stroke. Do you think it could be a moisture issue in the walnut, or maybe something with how I'm orienting the board on the bench?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That's a good point about examining the chisel edge. A moisture issue in the walnut could be contributing to the problem, as it may be causing the wood fibers to resist that last tiny bit of pressure. It's also possible that the board's orientation on the bench is affecting how the fibers are shifting during the cut. Try clamping the board to the bench in a orientation that invites the fibers to move towards the chisel, and see if that improves the fit.",
      "source": "target",
